mach-o/dyld.h: define DYLD_EXCLAVEKIT_UNAVAILABLE, which the published copy uses
but never defines.

Apple's release process deletes `#ifndef __OPEN_SOURCE__` regions on the way
out of the tree.  In mach-o/dyld.h that region is the whole definition:

    #ifndef __OPEN_SOURCE__
     #define DYLD_EXCLAVEKIT_UNAVAILABLE
    #endif //__OPEN_SOURCE__

The eight declarations that use the macro were not deleted with it, so
dyld-1378's mach-o/dyld.h does not compile on its own -- `expected function
body after function declarator`, eight times, at columns 208 to 234.  Apple's
SDK copy has the region; the published one does not.

Restoring it as an empty macro is exactly what Apple's own copy expands to:
ExclaveKit is not a platform we build for, and this SDK emits no availability
diagnostics regardless (sdk/local/AvailabilityInternal.h).

--- a/mach-o/dyld.h
+++ b/mach-o/dyld.h
@@ -40,6 +40,11 @@
  #define DYLD_DRIVERKIT_UNAVAILABLE
 #endif
 
+/* machorun: see sdk/patches/0001-dyld-exclavekit-unavailable.patch */
+#ifndef DYLD_EXCLAVEKIT_UNAVAILABLE
+ #define DYLD_EXCLAVEKIT_UNAVAILABLE
+#endif
+
 
